export const crossStraitLink=`<button id="cross-strait-open" class="korean-article-link" type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="cross-strait-info"><strong>Taiwan and cross-strait relations</strong><span>History, cultural ties, government, and international representation.</span></button>`;
export const crossStraitDialog=`<dialog id="cross-strait-info" class="korean-reader" aria-labelledby="cross-strait-title"><header class="korean-reader-header"><h2 id="cross-strait-title">Taiwan and cross-strait relations</h2><button id="cross-strait-close" type="button" aria-label="Close article">×</button></header><article class="korean-reader-body"><p class="korean-article-lead">Taiwan and mainland China share deep historical and cultural ties. Separate governments administer the two sides of the Taiwan Strait.</p>
<p><small>Reviewed 26 October 2026</small></p>
<section><h3>Names and government</h3>
<p>The People’s Republic of China (PRC), established in 1949, governs mainland China. Its capital is Beijing.</p>
<p>The government based in Taipei uses the name Republic of China (ROC). The ROC was founded in 1912; its central government relocated to Taiwan in 1949 during the Chinese Civil War. Today it administers Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen, Matsu, and several smaller islands. It operates its own elections, courts, armed forces, and currency. <a href="https://www.ey.gov.tw/state/62879155A536D543/bf75db05-30af-4c3a-bdda-3fe32e3f8e5a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROC Executive Yuan: historical background</a>; <a href="https://english.president.gov.tw/page/93" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROC constitutional framework</a>.</p></section>
<section><h3>How the present division developed</h3>
<p>Taiwan was home to Indigenous societies long before the governments discussed here. During the seventeenth century, Dutch and Spanish forces established colonial settlements on parts of the island. In 1662, Zheng Chenggong, also known as Koxinga, expelled the Dutch and established a base for resistance to the Qing dynasty. His successors ruled until Qing forces defeated them in 1683. The Qing incorporated Taiwan into Fujian’s administration the following year. <a href="https://pages.ucsd.edu/~dkjordan/tw/TaiwanHistoryTable.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David K. Jordan, UC San Diego: historical chronology</a>.</p>
<p>Migration from Fujian and Guangdong strengthened Taiwan’s links with the mainland during Qing rule. Taiwan became a separate province in 1885. A decade later, Qing China’s defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895) forced it to accept the Treaty of Shimonoseki. Under that treaty, China ceded Taiwan and Penghu to Japan. Japanese colonial rule lasted until 1945. <a href="https://www.jacar.go.jp/english/exhibition/jacarbl-fsjwar-e/about/p005.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Japan Center for Asian Historical Records: war and the cession of Taiwan</a>.</p>
<p>Japan’s surrender in the Second World War brought ROC administration to Taiwan in 1945. The ROC had been founded on the mainland in 1912, while Taiwan was under Japanese rule. The Chinese Civil War then resumed between the Nationalists and Communists. Following Communist victory on the mainland in 1949, Mao Zedong proclaimed the PRC, and the Nationalist-led ROC government relocated to Taiwan. The two governments continued to claim to represent China, establishing the division across the strait that underlies today’s dispute. <a href="https://www.ey.gov.tw/state/62879155A536D543/bf75db05-30af-4c3a-bdda-3fe32e3f8e5a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROC Executive Yuan: historical background</a>; <a href="https://mu.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/sgxw/202208/t20220810_10739905.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRC State Council: historical account</a>.</p>
<p>Taiwan subsequently moved from authoritarian Nationalist rule toward multiparty democracy. Martial law ended in 1987, and voters directly elected the president for the first time in 1996. In 1987, authorities also permitted family visits to the mainland, reopening contact after decades of separation. Personal, commercial, and cultural exchanges expanded alongside the continuing political dispute. <a href="https://web.cec.gov.tw/english/article/24" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Central Election Commission: democratic development</a>; <a href="https://www.mac.gov.tw/en/cp.aspx?n=33F7946C1005555F" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mainland Affairs Council: cross-strait exchanges</a>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Cultural ties</h3>
<p>Taiwan forms part of the Sinosphere, the wider cultural sphere shaped by Chinese writing and traditions. These connections are visible in its use of Chinese characters, Mandarin, religious traditions, and family ties with the mainland. Taiwan is also an important center for Chinese-language scholarship and cultural life. <a href="https://taiwanfellowship.ncl.edu.tw/files/scholar_publish/2495-esyhmcpjhedrgce.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julia G. Bentley: Taiwan and the Sinosphere</a>.</p>
<p>Most of Taiwan’s population is of Han Chinese ancestry, including Hoklo and Hakka communities. Indigenous Austronesian peoples have their own languages and traditions, and more recent immigration has added further cultural influences. This shared Chinese heritage coexists with distinct local experiences and a range of personal and political identities. <a href="https://ws.mofa.gov.tw/001/Upload/402/relfile/20/422/1c9181eb-7df6-42e3-8222-45288d32fe73.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Taiwan Snapshot: population and culture</a>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Political positions</h3>
<p>The PRC’s one-China principle holds that Taiwan is part of China and that the PRC is the sole legal government representing China. Beijing seeks national reunification. Its stated policy favors peaceful reunification while retaining the option of force under specified circumstances. <a href="https://mu.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/sgxw/202208/t20220810_10739905.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRC State Council: 2022 white paper</a>.</p>
<p>The ROC continues to operate under its own constitution and institutions. Taiwan’s political debate includes different views on Chinese identity, relations with Beijing, and the island’s future. The governing authorities in Taipei reject the PRC’s authority to represent Taiwan internationally. <a href="https://english.president.gov.tw/page/93" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROC constitutional framework</a>; <a href="https://www.mac.gov.tw/en/News_Content.aspx?n=1C6028CA080A27B3&s=62970118FECC97F9&sms=6F070A2443531120" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mainland Affairs Council: stated position</a>.</p></section>
<section><h3>International representation</h3>
<p>In 1971, UN General Assembly Resolution 2758 recognized the PRC government’s representatives as China’s representatives at the United Nations. It removed the representatives of Chiang Kai-shek. <a href="https://ask.un.org/faq/320138" target="_blank" rel="noopener">United Nations: Resolution 2758 and its history</a>.</p>
<p>Interpretations of the resolution’s implications for Taiwan differ. Beijing maintains that it settled representation for all of China, including Taiwan. Taipei maintains that it addressed China’s UN seat and left Taiwan’s status outside its scope. <a href="https://un.china-mission.gov.cn/eng/zgyw/202509/t20250930_11721842.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRC position paper</a>; <a href="https://www.mac.gov.tw/en/News_Content.aspx?n=1C6028CA080A27B3&s=62970118FECC97F9&sms=6F070A2443531120" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Taipei’s position</a>.</p>
<p>Diplomatic recognition and practical relations also differ. The European Union, for example, recognizes the PRC government as China’s sole legal government while maintaining extensive unofficial relations with Taiwan. Governments define their own one-China policies; their wording and commitments vary. <a href="https://www.eeas.europa.eu/delegations/taiwan/european-union-and-taiwan_en?s=242" target="_blank" rel="noopener">European Union: relations with Taiwan</a>.</p></section>
<section><h3>How this atlas presents the region</h3>
<p>The atlas groups Taiwan within its China map and displays the counties, cities, districts, and townships administered by the ROC. This navigation groups places for exploration. Administrative boundaries, cultural connections, and competing sovereignty claims are described separately in this article.</p></section></article></dialog>`;
export function wireCrossStraitInfo(){
 const dialog=document.getElementById('cross-strait-info');
 document.getElementById('cross-strait-open').onclick=()=>dialog.showModal();
 document.getElementById('cross-strait-close').onclick=()=>dialog.close();
 dialog.addEventListener('click',event=>{if(event.target===dialog){const r=dialog.getBoundingClientRect();if(event.clientX<r.left||event.clientX>r.right||event.clientY<r.top||event.clientY>r.bottom)dialog.close();}});
 dialog.addEventListener('close',()=>{dialog.querySelector('article').scrollTop=0;});
 return {close(){if(dialog.open)dialog.close();}};
}
